<script lang="ts">export let task: string;</script>
<div class="section-heading"><div><h1>{task==='timing'?'Word timing':'Waqf segmentation'} scores</h1><p>What the numbers mean and how they are calculated.</p></div></div>
<article class="metrics">
{#if task==='segmentation'}
  <section><h2>Segments F1</h2><p>Does the system split the recording at the reciter’s audible stops (waqf), returning complete recitation segments with usable boundaries? A segment is correct when both boundaries fit one reviewed segment, at least half its speech duration is retained, and it includes no more than 750 ms of other speech.</p><p>Missing, splitting, or merging recitation segments reduces the score. Matching is one-to-one: several outputs cannot each claim credit for the same reference segment.</p><div class="formula">Segments found = matched segments / reference Quran segments<br/>Segments correct = matched segments / evaluated output segments<br/>Segments F1 = 2 × matched segments / (reference segments + evaluated output segments)</div></section>
  <section><h2>Boundaries F1</h2><p>How often are the starts and ends in the right places? Starts match starts and ends match ends, one-to-one. This gives partial credit when one edge is good but the whole segment is not usable.</p><div class="formula">Boundaries F1 = 2 × matched boundaries / (reference boundaries + evaluated output boundaries)</div><p>More metrics shows the share of reference boundaries found and the share of predicted boundaries that are correct.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Accepted boundaries</h2><p>A start may be up to 750 ms late; an end may be up to 750 ms early. Up to two seconds of extra padding is allowed into silence. Neighbouring speech limits that padding to at most 750 ms into the neighbouring word or segment.</p><div class="formula">max(0, start − 2, previous speech end − 0.75) ≤ predicted start ≤ start + 0.75<br/>end − 0.75 ≤ predicted end ≤ min(audio duration, end + 2, next speech start + 0.75)<br/>predicted duration ≥ reference duration / 2</div><p>Absent neighbours impose no bound. Total overlap with annotated non-Quran speech and standalone formulas must be at most 750 ms for a complete segment match.</p><p>Only Quran-bearing reference segments are targets. Outputs touching Quran are evaluated in full. Non-Quran-only outputs are ignored; silence-only outputs within the target boundary windows count as extra predictions. Outputs outside those regions earn no credit or penalty.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Processing speed</h2><p>The leaderboard shows audio processed per second: 10× realtime means one minute of audio took six seconds. Higher is faster. The exact real-time factor remains visible underneath; 10× realtime equals RTF 0.1. Runtime is self-reported, so compare CPU/GPU and hardware details. Report runtime for all recordings or none.</p></section>
{:else}
  <section><h2>Words timed correctly</h2><p>For each supplied word, are its start and end both within 300 ms of the reviewed reference? Higher means more words timed well enough for practical use. The reference uses its existing continuous-recitation padding convention.</p><div class="formula">Word error = max(|predicted start − reference start|, |predicted end − reference end|)<br/>Word passes when error ≤ 0.300 seconds<br/>Words timed correctly = passing words / all supplied words</div><p>Words are paired by position, with no text recognition or reference matching to score. Missing timings count as failures. Formula clips use the same timing test.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Clean clips</h2><p>How often can the complete clip be used without correcting a word? A clip passes only when every word passes the 300 ms test. Longer clips naturally have more opportunities for an error, so this complements the word score.</p><div class="formula">Clean clips = clips with every word passing / all supplied clips</div><p><code>words: null</code> marks every word in that clip as untimed. Individual null entries mark individual failures.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Detailed diagnostics</h2><p>The downloadable report also scores 50, 100, 200, and 500 ms tolerances. Mean boundary error is the average absolute start/end error, in milliseconds; it is undefined if any word is untimed. These diagnostics do not affect rank. Runtime is not scored for this task.</p></section>
{/if}
  <section><h2>Ranking and filters</h2><p>{task==='timing'?'Words timed correctly':'Segments F1'} determines rank, with ties sharing rank. Higher quality scores are better. Counts are pooled across selected recordings before computing ratios; recording percentages are never averaged. Filters and single-recording comparisons use the same scorer. “—” means undefined or unreported.</p><p>User-facing parameters is the self-reported number of result-affecting demo controls or documented package options, excluding internal developer settings. Fewer may be simpler; more may offer flexibility.</p></section>
</article>
